== 1. the tree stamps, resolved — not read off the logs ==
`dirty:` / `tree:` stamps are NOT git objects; they are @bisellium/shim's sourceTreeHash over the
repo minus [studio, .bisellium, examples/] (studio/bisellium.yml's source_excludes). I recomputed
them rather than trusting them:

  9eff23d (master, pre-W-057)  -> 0bc65b48b76430f9484316d86a742f76fc8305f6
  e077ccb (the fix commit)     -> 8b38f8b84e95f49b5e0eeceea1cc322ee5f2a801
  HEAD    (c5776cd)            -> 8b38f8b84e95f49b5e0eeceea1cc322ee5f2a801

So: all seven reds stamp `dirty:0bc65b48…` = HEAD at the pre-W-057 commit; the three probationes
certify `tree:8b38f8b8…` = the fix commit's source tree, which is also the current tree. The
certificates name the source tree under review, exactly.

IMPORTANT, and it bears on disclosure 1: verify.ts computes the hash at a COMMIT, not over the
working tree (verify.ts:160, and its own honest comment at :166-169). A `dirty:<hash>` stamp
therefore CANNOT distinguish a pre-fix working tree from a post-fix one. The stamps alone do not
establish that reds 1-6 ran against unmodified source. I established that from log CONTENT instead
— see §3.

