== 6. sec-lead's Medium / BLOCKS-MERGE — MY RULING: real, but OUT OF SCOPE for W-057 ==
The claim: exception-derived text still reaches a response body through `withCapturedConsole`
(http.ts:275) -> `writeResponse` (http.ts:308-310) -> `sendJson` -> `res.end`, because the write
verbs console.error their own caught values.

It is real, and I reproduced it rather than agreeing on a reading. Executing exactly what
http.ts:275 and :308 do around runGreenlight against an unreadable opus file:

  BODY: {"ok":false,"exitCode":2,"output":"could not read /tmp/…/opera/W-002.md: EACCES:
         permission denied, open '/tmp/…/opera/W-002.md'"}

An absolute path and an exception message, in a 200 response body. Source: writes.ts:797.
sec-lead's file:line chain for handoff/answer/greenlight/budget/talk/delegate checks out.

RULING: not W-057's, and not fixable inside W-057. Five reasons, in order of weight:

  1. It is a DIFFERENT CHANNEL, and a DOCUMENTED ONE. docs/ADOPTION.md:908-915 specifies
     `POST /api/*` as "always HTTP 200 with { ok, exitCode, output } for a completed command,
     success or refusal alike", and names `output` as the client's only way to tell success from
     refusal. Returning CLI output IS the contract. Suppressing it is a contract change with a
     console-UX consequence (apps/web's postWrite reads it), which is a design decision, not a
     security patch — exactly the shape of thing the brief's own Out of scope reserves.
  2. It is a DIFFERENT TRUST BOUNDARY. Every one of those six routes is in WRITE_PATHS: token +
     origin + Content-Type gated (http.ts:651-657). The flow GHAS flagged is unauthenticated —
     I confirmed one live: `GET /api/opus/%` needs no token, throws URIError from
     decodeURIComponent, and reaches route()'s catch. Post-fix it answers
     {"error":"internal error","ref":"ee80c5ec-…"}. That is the alert's class, and it is closed.
  3. It is ZERO-DELTA. `git diff master...HEAD -- packages/commands/` is empty. This diff neither
     created nor widened the channel; the pre-existing risk is identical before and after.
  4. Fixing it here would BREAK THE BRIEF. packages/commands/ is not in Files owned; the brief
     says in terms that a wider diff is itself the finding ("if the diff edits either, the change
     was wider than specified"). A censor who demands an out-of-scope edit to pass is asking the
     builder to violate the spec it was reviewed against.
  5. The brief's own census was scoped, explicitly, to "every error response body in http.ts" —
     bodies carrying an `error` key. `{ok, exitCode, output}` is not an error body and never
     entered the 17-site census.

BUT the brief overclaims, and that is a SPEC defect worth carrying forward. Design §3 says dropping
the parse message makes it "the only remaining path by which an arbitrary exception's text reaches a
response body", and §"The class, not the instance" says "two response sinks" measured across
http.ts. With `writeResponse` on the table both statements are false as written. The successor opus's
brief must not inherit them. Filed as finding 1 below, FILE-AS-OPUS, sec-lead credited.

