== 8. findings ==

1. [Medium · FILE-AS-OPUS · spec, not code] The brief's "only remaining path" and "two response
   sinks" claims are false as written: `writeResponse`'s `{output}` is a third, documented
   exception-text channel on six token-gated write routes. Not W-057's to close (§6). The successor
   brief must state the census's scope as "bodies carrying an `error` key" rather than "any
   response body". sec-lead found it; reproduced above. ONE instance of the claim, one channel.

2. [Low · advisory · CLASS, 3 instances, all enumerated] Wall-clock sleeps stand in for a seam —
   a recurrence of L-035 (tests×wall-clock-sleep-for-a-missing-seam, retro 32). I searched every
   `+` line of the test diff for `setTimeout`; these three are all of them:
     a. server.test.ts induceRequestStreamError — `setTimeout(…, 150)` before the destroy
     b. server.test.ts behaviour 6 — `await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 200))` "let the log land"
     c. server.test.ts behaviour 7 — `await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 300))` after opening 51 SSE clients
   (b) and (c) have a seam available in the same file: `waitFor(predicate)` at :209. (c) is the
   flake-prone one — it gates a 503 assertion on 51 connections being registered in 300ms. It did
   not flake in 4 runs here. Advisory, not blocking: the file already carries 13 pre-existing
   sleeps, so this is house convention, and holding an opus for a convention its own file
   establishes is not a censor's job. Named so the class does not go quiet.

3. [Low · advisory · instance only] The brief required the closedness line's location to be named
   IN THE COMMIT ("put it wherever typecheck covers, and say where in the commit", Files owned).
   The commit message says the line exists and that tsc enforces it, but never says where. It is
   at apps/server/src/http.ts:140 — in src, not in the test file the Files owned list guessed.
   Recording the location here discharges the intent; the instruction was still not followed.

4. [Low · advisory · spec defect, correctly resolved by the builder] Behaviour 7's table maps
   `:671` to "/api/opus/:id for an absent id". It is not: master:671 is the `/api/_poll`
   NODE_ENV guard's 404, and the absent-opus case is `:609`, which the table lists separately.
   The builder covered the actual `:671` site (toggling NODE_ENV and restoring it in a `finally`)
   and `:609` independently, so coverage is complete despite the mislabel. Noted so a future
   reading of the table does not "fix" a correct test.

5. [Informational] `scripts/w057-behaviour7-mutation-check.mjs` is not in the brief's Files owned,
   though the brief's own behaviour-7 instruction ("give it a mutation red") and the standing
   "mechanical work is a script you write, run and keep" rule together compel it. `bisellium check`
   raised nothing (probatio.declared did not fire). Five sibling scripts already live in scripts/.
   Files owned should have named it; no action needed on this opus.

6. [Informational · refines the builder's own disclosure 4] The disclosure says a clean `.end()`
   "instead" trips Node's own clientError 400, so the induction "must be a hard .destroy(), never
   a graceful close". Measured, both modes, same server:
     MODE=fin      RESPONSE="HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n"
     MODE=destroy  RESPONSE=""
   and BOTH wrote `bisellium serve: 500 <ref> aborted` + stack to stderr. So it is not "instead":
   FIN takes this opus's 500 path too, and Node's 400 is bodyless — zero bytes of body, so it
   cannot narrate a stream error under either close. The property holds either way and `.destroy()`
   is not load-bearing. The disclosure's mechanism is right, its exclusivity is not.

== 9. rulings on the four builder disclosures ==

  1. Behaviour 7's red recorded AFTER implementation. UPHELD — the brief instructs it, twice.
     Reds section: "Behaviour 7 is the exception — it passes today by construction, being a
     regression guard for what must not change. Give it a mutation red (delete one of the three
     literal codes and watch it fail) rather than bundling it." Behaviour 7 itself: "The regression
     guard for a change that rewrites seventeen call sites." A mutation of a literal in the
     ErrorCode union is only possible once the union exists, so post-implementation is not a
     shortcut, it is the only order the instruction admits. Two caveats, neither fatal: the brief
     says "delete one of the three literal codes" (a residue of revision 3's five-row era; three
     is not a number this revision uses) and the script substitutes a literal rather than deleting
     one — deleting a union member would fail typecheck, not the byte-identity row, so the
     substitution is the correct reading of the intent. And red 07's `dirty:` stamp is structurally
     incapable of distinguishing pre- from post-implementation (§1), which is exactly why the
     builder disclosed it rather than letting the stamp imply otherwise. That is the behaviour the
     "evidence is produced, never backfilled" rule wants: the red WAS produced, by a script that is
     in the tree and that I re-ran.

  2. Behaviour 3's compile-time halves live in typecheck, not npm test. UPHELD — the brief puts
     them there. Files owned: "the @ts-expect-error closedness line (it belongs in a type-checked
     file; put it wherever typecheck covers, and say where in the commit)". Acceptance 1:
     "typecheck is not a formality here — it is the mechanism enforcing the invariant (behaviour 3),
     so a green typecheck with the ErrorCode union in place is itself an acceptance artefact." A
     passing test file cannot assert a compile error; only the compiler can, and mutations 4b and
     4d show it does. The "convention-matched to the sibling mutation scripts" half of the
     disclosure I neither need nor grant — the brief's own text is sufficient authority, and the
     siblings (w030/w041/w044/w049/w079) are about red COMMANDS not being `npm test`, which is a
     different question. Docked to finding 3: the "say where in the commit" clause was not met.

  3. `:441` unreachable end-to-end, isPathContained exercised directly. UPHELD, and I verified it
     myself rather than accepting the claim. Seven traversal encodings over raw sockets, so no
     client library could normalise for me:
       /assets/../../../../etc/passwd            -> 404 {"error":"not found"}
       /assets/%2e%2e/%2e%2e/%2e%2e/etc/passwd   -> 404 {"error":"not found"}
       /assets/..%2f..%2f..%2fetc%2fpasswd       -> 404 {"error":"not found"}
       /assets/....//....//etc/passwd            -> 404 {"error":"not found"}
       /assets/.%2e/.%2e/etc/passwd              -> 404 {"error":"not found"}
       /assets/..%5c..%5cetc                     -> 404 {"error":"not found"}
       /assets/%2E%2E%2F%2E%2E%2Fetc             -> 404 {"error":"not found"}
     Not one reaches the 403. `new URL(req.url, …)` at http.ts:643 collapses every double-dot form
     the WHATWG spec recognises (including the %2e spellings) before serveStatic sees `pathname`.
     The brief's allowance is explicit — "if a path cannot be reached from a test, say so in the
     evidence rather than dropping it silently, because an unreachable error body is its own
     finding" — and the row is not dropped: it asserts isPathContained refuses the sibling-prefix
     escape, and the test comment states the unreachability at the row. Note this was ALREADY
     established in the tree at W-016 (server.test.ts:805-810 says the same thing in the same
     words), so the builder restated a known fact rather than discovering a convenient one.

  4. Behaviour 6's empirical finding cannot be misread as the fix. UPHELD on the question asked,
     with a correction to the finding itself (finding 6). It cannot be misread, for three reasons
     that do not depend on the reader's care: the note lives in a TEST helper's docstring
     (induceRequestStreamError), not in http.ts where a fix would live; it says in terms "a
     different, unrelated mechanism this opus has no business asserting on"; and the assertion it
     sits above is on the stderr `bisellium serve: 500 <ref> aborted` line, so nothing in the
     behaviour asserts on a 400 at all. Nobody can read it as W-057 having produced the 400.
     The correction: the note's "instead" is wrong — a clean FIN takes BOTH paths (measured, §8.6),
     and Node's 400 carries no body, which is a stronger result than the note claims, not a weaker
     one. The invariant "the message never becomes a response body" holds under both closes.

== 10. why this is a PASS and not a fail-with-fixes ==
Everything the spec asks for is present and measured: the choke point exists and is closed by the
compiler (4b, 4d), nobody routed around it and the pin proves it on the real file (4c), the 19-row
table asserts raw bytes with an added-field guard, six honest pre-fix reds plus a re-run mutation
red, the two pre-existing assertions untouched, no response body anywhere in a 5397-line suite log
carrying a path or exception text, and the alert's own unauthenticated flow verified closed against
a live server. The six findings are one spec overclaim to carry forward, one convention the file
itself established, two documentation nits and two informational notes. None of them is a defect in
the code under review, and none can be fixed inside this opus's declared files.

Three acceptance criteria (2, 3, 5) are external and cannot be satisfied by anyone in this repo
before the PR's CodeQL run. They are merge preconditions, not review failures, and they are the
coordinator's: record alert 3's number and fixed_at in the opus's evidence, check the FULL alert
list for a replacement js/stack-trace-exposure (the brief warns the flow can shift to a sibling —
both sinks are closed, so it should not, but acceptance 3 says check the list, not the alert), and
remove the merge gate's now-dead alert-3 exclusion, which otherwise silently swallows the next
stack-trace alert. Do not merge on this PASS alone until those three are done.
